<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">In dulci jubilo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words c. 1328; tune in a manuscript of c. 1400; first printed <a href="/years/1533">1533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;In dulci jubilo&quot; (&quot;In sweet rejoicing&quot;) is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> whose original words alternate between medieval German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, a mixed form called macaronic. They are thought to have been written around 1328 by the German mystic Heinrich Seuse.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>In dulci jubilo</b>&quot; (&quot;In sweet rejoicing&quot;) is a Christmas carol whose original words alternate between medieval German and Latin, a mixed form called macaronic.</blockquote><a href="/songs/in-dulci-jubilo">In dulci jubilo</a></li></ul></section>
